00:00:12.519 --> 00:00:18.419
Carl sat on his couch looking back and forth between his phone and the ceiling trying to figure out whether he was about to move

00:00:18.420 --> 00:00:25.769
Ten thousand miles away to Australia leaving his home girlfriend family friends job and whole life behind or not

00:00:26.410 --> 00:00:29.789
The opportunity to move had suddenly come up when his best friend, Novak

00:00:29.920 --> 00:00:33.540
Decided he would be moving to Australia with his girlfriend and aged 27,

00:00:33.670 --> 00:00:39.930
Novak was facing a sort of quarter life crisis and wanted to try and start something new and venture out into the world while he

00:00:39.930 --> 00:00:47.249
still felt new himself. Moving to Australia had been both Novak and Carl's dream ever since they were children and their families vacationed there.

00:00:47.410 --> 00:00:50.250
They both felt a weird natural connection with the colors,

00:00:50.590 --> 00:00:58.020
architecture, people and feeling of the country. They visited again several times after throughout their teens and early 20s. On one of their trips when

00:00:58.020 --> 00:01:03.509
they were 15, Carl and Novak agreed that they would move there when they were older. That, and they would start a business together

00:01:03.670 --> 00:01:07.500
This would remain their dream ever since as they grew older though,

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:10.650
like most dreams, it began to take a backseat to reality.

00:01:10.990 --> 00:01:12.159
Carl went to college for

00:01:12.159 --> 00:01:16.889
accounting in his hometown of Boston and got a job through a professor right after school that he couldn't turn down

00:01:17.409 --> 00:01:21.539
Novak had a family member in a tech startup company in Boston and as a result

00:01:21.540 --> 00:01:23.549
He was given an opportunity to work for the company

00:01:24.040 --> 00:01:27.059
However, after four years of Novak working for the company

00:01:27.060 --> 00:01:30.689
It was suddenly acquired and he was out of a job. As part of the layoffs,

00:01:30.689 --> 00:01:36.449
he was provided with the nice-sized severance package and now with some new extra money and nothing else stopping him,

00:01:36.450 --> 00:01:42.060
he decided he wanted to start a business and take a major leap of faith in his life. His girlfriend, somewhat of a free spirit,

00:01:42.220 --> 00:01:47.669
was on board with him. Novak came up with the idea of moving to Australia and creating an eco-friendly

00:01:48.100 --> 00:01:55.079
Agrotech company that utilized Australia's farming industry and its demand for local farm-fresh organic food. One month later

00:01:55.470 --> 00:01:59.639
Novak told Carl that he and his girlfriend were moving to Australia within the next couple months

00:01:59.799 --> 00:02:05.159
He told him about the business idea that he and his girlfriend worked on together and showed him the Beta website and app

00:02:05.159 --> 00:02:08.139
he created. It looked and sounded great and well thought-out.

00:02:08.720 --> 00:02:13.899
Novak's girlfriend had experience working in logistics and Novak understood the business and technology side.

00:02:13.970 --> 00:02:17.169
They were the perfect duo. When Karl didn't fully understand,

00:02:17.450 --> 00:02:22.509
Novak explained to him that he wanted him to be a partner in the business and that he should move to Australia with them.

00:02:22.690 --> 00:02:27.309
He told Karl it would be perfect and he could initially be in charge of sales, marketing, outreach,

00:02:27.680 --> 00:02:28.640
budgeting and all the

00:02:28.640 --> 00:02:34.750
finances. He reminded Karl of their dream and told him how it would only get harder to move and try to create something like this.

00:02:34.849 --> 00:02:40.719
He sort of ridiculed the idea of Karl staying in Boston and working a corporate job for the rest of his life as if that's

00:02:40.720 --> 00:02:41.739
what he really wanted

00:02:41.739 --> 00:02:44.349
He told Karl how this business had a real chance to help people,

00:02:44.480 --> 00:02:50.170
how they could build a business model that would provide families with food who couldn't afford it and help the planet by contributing to a

00:02:50.170 --> 00:02:56.619
more sustainable food industry. Karl agreed with Novak's points, but told him that he needed a couple days to think about it at least

00:02:57.140 --> 00:03:00.279
Novak agreed and told them to let him know after he thought about it more.

00:03:00.650 --> 00:03:04.690
Over the next day, Karl thought about all the factors associated with the decision.

00:03:04.690 --> 00:03:10.690
He thought about how his mother had recently passed away just two years prior and he and his father had become extremely close ever since

00:03:10.880 --> 00:03:12.489
His father was not taking it

00:03:12.489 --> 00:03:16.989
Well and Karl's older brother had a strained relationship with his father and so, in a way,

00:03:17.120 --> 00:03:23.200
Karl was all his father had left he worried that if he left, it would break him. Karl also thought about his girlfriend Stephanie

00:03:23.390 --> 00:03:25.390
They had only been dating a little more than a year,

00:03:25.430 --> 00:03:28.989
but he had already started imagining a future with her. Things were going well

00:03:28.989 --> 00:03:30.459
and they just seemed to click right?

00:03:30.459 --> 00:03:32.410
He knew that he couldn't expect her to go with him

00:03:32.410 --> 00:03:36.100
and he knew a long-distance relationship was bound to fail, if she even agreed to it.

00:03:36.200 --> 00:03:40.539
He thought about all his other friends and family. He knew it wasn't as if they would cease to exist,

00:03:40.910 --> 00:03:44.979
but he also knew that in a way their existence would fade a little perhaps so much

00:03:44.980 --> 00:03:48.100
so for some that it would almost be as if they no longer existed at all.

00:03:48.380 --> 00:03:49.940
Then he thought about his current job

00:03:49.940 --> 00:03:54.819
He worked for a great firm with great benefits, and he'd finally started making some headway in the company

00:03:54.819 --> 00:03:58.958
There was a lot of financial promise ahead of him and he knew it was the more prudent career bet,

00:03:59.209 --> 00:04:04.358
but then at the same time he thought about how a greater risk meant a potentially greater reward

00:04:04.430 --> 00:04:08.049
He didn't mind his current job, but it was fairly tedious and unexcited

00:04:08.120 --> 00:04:11.679
He sort of just happened into it as a means of a conservative career path

00:04:11.680 --> 00:04:18.130
He had always dreamed of being a part of building a business and creating a more meaningful impact and legacy for himself. Furthermore,

00:04:18.130 --> 00:04:22.989
he never particularly liked Boston. He had always wanted to move to the countryside ever since he was a kid

00:04:23.539 --> 00:04:26.529
Specifically he always envisioned a future where he lived in Australia

00:04:26.530 --> 00:04:30.579
It was just one of those things that he had convinced himself of and now at 27,

00:04:30.800 --> 00:04:35.109
Karl felt an urge to leave home and forge his own life for himself while he was still young

00:04:35.330 --> 00:04:41.109
He wondered how he could turn down such an opportunity to move to his dream location with his best friend and try to build something

00:04:41.110 --> 00:04:42.849
potentially important and helpful

00:04:42.849 --> 00:04:46.599
How could he turn his back on his own vision of his future when it was right in front of him?

00:04:46.819 --> 00:04:54.699
For the following three days Karl thought about the decision every waking second, ruminating back and forth between the potential outcomes of both choices after

00:04:54.919 --> 00:04:59.348
Four days, it seemed as if no matter how hard he tried he made no progress in the decision

00:04:59.349 --> 00:05:05.829
He remained equally unsure as to what he should do on the fourth day as the first both options seemed impossible to decipher

00:05:06.139 --> 00:05:14.079
It wasn't an equation with numeric values or obvious reasoning each option was completely different with their own entirely different set of variables and values,

00:05:14.080 --> 00:05:16.300
with no ability to know what any of it equated to

00:05:16.879 --> 00:05:19.959
Karl had purposely held off sharing the situation with anyone else,

00:05:19.960 --> 00:05:23.979
so to avoid getting anyone upset before he had a better sense of what he was going to do himself,

00:05:24.169 --> 00:05:26.679
but by this point he needed someone else's opinion.

00:05:27.050 --> 00:05:32.199
Over the next two days, Karl told his father his girlfriend two of his other close friends and his aunt.

00:05:32.389 --> 00:05:34.718
He asked each of them what they thought he should do.

00:05:35.210 --> 00:05:36.949
Everyone gave him different answers.

00:05:36.949 --> 00:05:41.949
His dad and one of his friends said he should go his aunt and his other friend said he should stay.

00:05:42.050 --> 00:05:44.000
His girlfriend didn't give him a straight answer,

00:05:44.000 --> 00:05:47.619
but her tears said everything Karl needed to know. They all had perfectly good,

00:05:47.900 --> 00:05:51.940
rational explanations for what they suggested and yet Karl was no better off

00:05:52.280 --> 00:05:58.720
He wondered how each answer could sound equally reasonable, but be completely contradictory. On the sixth night of indecision,

00:05:58.900 --> 00:06:01.440
Karl went on a walk around the city to try and clear his head.

00:06:01.629 --> 00:06:08.349
He walked late into the night, all around the city and along the harbour eventually he found himself on a bench facing the water completely

00:06:08.689 --> 00:06:11.019
secluded in a sectioned off corner of a pathway.

00:06:11.210 --> 00:06:12.740
He sat, deep in thought.

00:06:12.740 --> 00:06:19.299
He thought about how his entire future was hinged on this one decision contained inside his head, that in his brain was the power to

00:06:19.339 --> 00:06:24.909
facilitate one move that would contort his entire world forever and yet he couldn't even know what choice was right.

00:06:25.009 --> 00:06:29.109
He thought about how insane this was to be given the ability to make decisions

00:06:29.149 --> 00:06:32.169
but not given the ability to ever know the consequences of them

00:06:32.169 --> 00:06:35.588
He felt like he was blind and forced to drive a car. He wished

00:06:35.589 --> 00:06:38.889
he could just know how both options would play out. Then he would know which one to choose.

00:06:39.860 --> 00:06:45.819
Overwhelmed, Karl looked out at the stars and moon glimmering against the water. He closed his eyes and took a deep breath

00:06:46.639 --> 00:06:52.508
Suddenly an older man walked past Carl and sat on a bench just a couple feet away. This jolted Carl to attention

00:06:52.580 --> 00:06:53.539
He was immediately

00:06:53.539 --> 00:06:59.109
surprised by anyone being in the same secluded pathway and was curious as to why a man who looked to be at least in his

00:06:59.110 --> 00:07:01.149
80s would be out, alone, at 2:00 a.m.

00:07:01.729 --> 00:07:07.028
Worried that the man was perhaps unwell or lost, Karl decided to ask him if he was okay. Politely,

00:07:07.029 --> 00:07:14.079
he turned and leaned over to the man and said "How's everything going tonight?" The man slowly turned towards Carl and said "Good.

00:07:14.149 --> 00:07:16.039
How 'bout yourself?" "Good,"

00:07:16.039 --> 00:07:20.348
Karl responded, "is everything okay?" "Yeah, everything's great. It's a beautiful night,

00:07:20.349 --> 00:07:23.319
isn't it?" replied the old man. Karl looked up to the sky.

00:07:23.749 --> 00:07:26.439
"Yeah, it is." he agreed, then

00:07:26.439 --> 00:07:29.498
they sat quietly as Carl sort of jumped back and forth between

00:07:29.959 --> 00:07:36.429
contemplating his decision again and being distracted by the man's strange presence the idea of asking the man his opinion occurred to Karl.

00:07:36.649 --> 00:07:40.479
He looked over to the man and asked him if he could ask him a question. "Yeah, sure.

00:07:40.509 --> 00:07:44.109
Shoot." the man replied. Karl then went on and described his predicament

00:07:44.180 --> 00:07:49.180
He explained the business, his job, his girlfriend, his best friend, his father and everything else.

00:07:49.249 --> 00:07:53.259
Then he asked the old man what he would do. The man laughed a little, as if he had some

00:07:53.389 --> 00:07:56.318
experience or insight about the subject that was comical to him.

00:07:56.569 --> 00:08:01.449
"So, you want to make the right choice, but you don't know what the right choice is?" the old man asked.

00:08:01.449 --> 00:08:04.568
"Yeah, exactly." Karl replied. "I'm completely lost.

00:08:04.809 --> 00:08:07.449
I wish I could just know what both would be like, you know?" "Yeah,

00:08:07.449 --> 00:08:11.889
if only." the old man said with another chuckle. "Wouldn't that make everything easier?

00:08:12.229 --> 00:08:18.128
They sat quietly for a moment while the man thought. Then he said, "What if you just decide right now based on what I choose?

00:08:18.259 --> 00:08:24.398
Someone who basically doesn't know you at all. It'll be like flipping a coin, but the coin has a brain and understands the situation a little."

00:08:24.979 --> 00:08:28.658
Somehow this made sense to Karl, and after thinking for a moment, he agreed.

00:08:28.659 --> 00:08:33.549
He just needed it to be over. The old man paused and then quickly said, "Alright,

00:08:33.550 --> 00:08:37.750
you're going." In a desperate clinging to reason, Karl asked the old man,

00:08:38.029 --> 00:08:39.189
"What made you choose that?"

00:08:39.189 --> 00:08:42.519
"I guess you'll have to find that out." replied the old man.

00:08:43.010 --> 00:08:46.899
Karl sat for a moment processing the fact that he was going to do what the man just said.

00:08:47.380 --> 00:08:50.200
Then, when he got up and went to thank the man and wish him a good night,

00:08:50.270 --> 00:08:54.520
he was gone. Karl figured he must have just left while he was thinking and didn't hear him say goodbye.

00:08:55.340 --> 00:09:02.019
Five weeks later Carl was on a plane with Novak and Novak's girlfriend, headed to Australia. 20 years would go by.

00:09:02.540 --> 00:09:08.200
Karl has remained in Australia ever since he moved. He now considers it home even after 20 years,

00:09:08.200 --> 00:09:11.979
it still feels amazing to him. A walk along the coast or into the countryside

00:09:12.230 --> 00:09:14.830
never ceases to amaze him and bring him a little peace.

00:09:14.990 --> 00:09:19.780
He and Stephanie broke up within the first three months of trying to maintain some form of a long-distance relationship.

00:09:20.660 --> 00:09:25.179
One and a half years later, he met a woman named Natalie. They fell in love almost instantly.

00:09:25.190 --> 00:09:26.770
They got married three years later,

00:09:26.770 --> 00:09:31.960
and they now have two healthy boys who have provided Karl's life with a great source of purpose and love.

00:09:32.330 --> 00:09:38.470
Karl, Novak and Novak's girlfriend's business ran for about six years until unforeseen industry changes,

00:09:38.930 --> 00:09:41.979
operational problems and regulations forced them to shut it down.

00:09:42.250 --> 00:09:47.950
They had tried pivoting the business model several times before it failed, all with no luck. During this time,

00:09:47.950 --> 00:09:48.440
however,

00:09:48.440 --> 00:09:51.789
Karl discovered a surprisingly strong passion for the farming process

00:09:51.980 --> 00:09:57.760
himself. A year after the original business ended, Karl bought a small indoor farm facility and started his own

00:09:57.980 --> 00:10:01.630
personal farming business. The business became profitable in the first three years

00:10:01.790 --> 00:10:06.040
but the profit ceiling is low and it brings in a fairly small amount of money each month.

00:10:06.260 --> 00:10:08.319
His wife, who works as a freelance copywriter,

00:10:08.870 --> 00:10:10.730
doesn't bring in much money either.

00:10:10.730 --> 00:10:17.950
Because of the low income and the expenses of two children, the financial burden made Karl and Natalie horrible and bitter to one another. Their

00:10:18.110 --> 00:10:22.840
personalities just never quite clicked after the early phases of the marriage. They've stayed together for the kids,

00:10:22.840 --> 00:10:29.199
but they're miserable. Karl and Novak have remained close during and after the business and have formed a nice little friend group with others

00:10:29.200 --> 00:10:34.390
they've met along the way even though the original plan didn't work out, Karl feels connected with his work and

00:10:34.610 --> 00:10:39.370
contributing directly to a local sustainable food industry gives him the occasional sense of pride.

00:10:39.740 --> 00:10:45.010
However, it has come at the cost of constant financial burden, heavy stress and minimal free time.

00:10:45.380 --> 00:10:50.949
Karl isn't happy but he isn't unhappy he's a mix of both, depending on the time or day. As

00:10:51.350 --> 00:10:58.209
the years went by, Karl visited home less and less. He would go home and visit his father most Christmases and special occasions,

00:10:58.209 --> 00:11:03.698
but eventually he started missing a few years here and there. The last time he visited his father was at his funeral.

00:11:04.339 --> 00:11:07.779
At the service, Karl stood, looking down at his father's lifeless body.

00:11:07.939 --> 00:11:13.209
He looked around at old friends, family members neighbors, his brother who he hadn't seen in five years.

00:11:13.399 --> 00:11:18.308
He looked at his wife. He immediately needed to get some fresh air after the service was over

00:11:18.309 --> 00:11:21.789
He walked to the bar down the street that he would go to when he was in his 20s

00:11:21.790 --> 00:11:27.790
As fate would have it, he passed by Stephanie. They noticed each other hugged and talked for a little while.

00:11:28.040 --> 00:11:32.139
Karl told her why he was home and she said she was sorry. It was weird.

00:11:32.139 --> 00:11:34.868
He felt at ease, almost as if they didn't miss a beat

00:11:35.089 --> 00:11:38.349
He felt more comfortable with her in that moment than he had in a long time.

00:11:39.049 --> 00:11:42.549
After a little, they said how great it was to see each other and went their separate ways.

00:11:43.069 --> 00:11:48.368
Karl sat at the bar drinking alone. He thought back to the day he left and felt a weird sense of regret.

00:11:48.589 --> 00:11:54.068
He wondered if he did the wrong thing, leaving his father, his family and Stephanie. Leaving his good job

00:11:54.069 --> 00:11:56.589
that would have certainly paid away most of his current problems.

00:11:56.660 --> 00:12:02.709
He remembered how he let some stranger make such an important decision for him and suddenly felt like his regret wasn't even his,

00:12:02.779 --> 00:12:04.779
which made it feel exponentially worse.

00:12:05.329 --> 00:12:08.859
Later that night, Karl walked around the city by himself to try to clear his head

00:12:09.230 --> 00:12:13.689
He walked down the edge of the city along the harbor. He sat down on the same bench

00:12:13.689 --> 00:12:18.639
he sat on many years ago, facing the water. He sat back in the bench looking up at the stars,

00:12:18.949 --> 00:12:22.238
closed his eyes and breathed in deeply. He sat there for a moment.

00:12:22.610 --> 00:12:29.199
Suddenly, an old man poked Karl's shoulder. Karl, disoriented, opened his eyes to an old man standing above him

00:12:29.480 --> 00:12:32.980
Before Carl could say anything, the old man asked him if he was okay.

00:12:33.319 --> 00:12:38.199
Karl, having realized he must have passed out on the bench, told the man he was fine and just fell asleep.

00:12:38.389 --> 00:12:43.599
The old man sat on the bench a few feet away as he explained to Carl how it was unsafe to fall asleep alone in

00:12:43.600 --> 00:12:48.669
such an area. "You're likely to get robbed or something." He said. Karl agreed and said it was an accident.

00:12:48.669 --> 00:12:52.569
He explained to the old man that he hadn't been able to get much sleep the last several days,

00:12:52.819 --> 00:12:57.479
because he had been trying to make the decision about whether or not he would be moving to Australia

00:12:58.060 --> 00:13:04.469
After talking for a little while and explaining his predicament about the business, his best friend, his girlfriend, his father and everything else,

00:13:04.720 --> 00:13:09.750
Karl had the idea to ask the old man what he thought he should do. The man laughed a little as if he had some

00:13:09.790 --> 00:13:13.889
experience or insight about the subject that was comical to him. After a little more conversation,

00:13:14.320 --> 00:13:19.979
the man said, "What if you just decide right now, based on what I choose? Someone who basically doesn't know you at all.

00:13:20.190 --> 00:13:24.389
It'll be like flipping a coin but the coin has a brain and understands the situation a little."

00:13:24.910 --> 00:13:27.870
Stricken with a weird sense of déjà vu, Karl agreed.

00:13:27.970 --> 00:13:33.060
He just needed it to be over. The old man paused for a moment and then quickly said "Alright,

00:13:33.060 --> 00:13:40.049
you're staying." In a desperate clinging to reason, Karl asked, "Why, what made you choose that?" "I guess you'll have to find that out."

00:13:40.510 --> 00:13:42.040
said the old man.

00:13:42.040 --> 00:13:47.940
Five weeks later Novak was on a plane for Australia while Karl ate dinner with Stephanie and his father.

00:13:48.640 --> 00:13:50.410
20 years would go by

00:13:50.410 --> 00:13:51.940
Karl has stayed in the Boston area.

00:13:51.940 --> 00:13:57.509
He has climbed the ranks in his career field and has become chief financial officer for a nice midsize company.

00:13:57.510 --> 00:13:59.510
He is making an incredible salary.

00:13:59.560 --> 00:14:02.310
He married Stephanie a couple years after deciding to stay.

00:14:02.410 --> 00:14:08.370
They decided against having children because Stephanie was against it and Karl wasn't sure himself and was willing to concede

00:14:08.710 --> 00:14:11.220
Over the years their love grew stronger and stronger.

00:14:11.440 --> 00:14:18.080
They fight like any couple but there's a luster in their relationship that's rare and always present. They seem to just click, as if their

00:14:18.320 --> 00:14:24.040
personalities were meant to work together. Their relationship has provided Carl with a great source of love and purpose in his life

00:14:24.700 --> 00:14:27.980
Karl had also remained close with his father and helped him into his old age.

00:14:28.150 --> 00:14:34.560
They've maintained a close bond that Karl cherishes. Most of Karl's other friends, however have moved away or drifted apart

00:14:35.110 --> 00:14:40.229
Novak and Carl sort of lost contact as well only seeing each other once every year or so, if that.

00:14:40.360 --> 00:14:46.050
With no children and a higher income, money has never really been an issue and his personal life is fairly stress-free

00:14:46.390 --> 00:14:50.370
He never really came around to liking his job, though, as he climbed the corporate ranks.

00:14:50.430 --> 00:14:58.860
It came with increasing bureaucratic tedium and he found an absurdity and pointlessness in it all. The longer he stayed though, the harder it was to leave. As he's aged,

00:14:58.860 --> 00:15:03.329
he's felt a sense of boredom, hollowness and social isolation in his life.

00:15:03.550 --> 00:15:10.669
He isn't happy but he isn't unhappy he's a mix of both, depending on the time or day. On his 40th birthday,

00:15:11.040 --> 00:15:12.450
Karl had a party.

00:15:12.450 --> 00:15:14.509
Novak flew out, along with a couple other old

00:15:14.610 --> 00:15:20.659
friends. At the party, Karl spent a good deal of time catching up with Novak and Novak told him all about the last couple years

00:15:20.660 --> 00:15:21.720
in Australia.

00:15:21.720 --> 00:15:25.250
He told him how his three kids were doing and how well the business was doing

00:15:25.470 --> 00:15:32.089
Karl asked Novak about some details in the business and Novak explained how their third partner who they had found when they first moved and

00:15:32.090 --> 00:15:34.090
had helped them get the business through some hard times,

00:15:34.320 --> 00:15:37.699
was now playing a key role in the business, moving into foreign markets.

00:15:37.800 --> 00:15:42.769
He told Karl the business was helping thousands of families each month, and he was extremely proud of it.

00:15:42.780 --> 00:15:47.060
Then he told karlie wished he would have been the person he was talking about instead. Karl

00:15:47.060 --> 00:15:51.679
saw the pride and enthusiasm in Novak's eyes when he spoke about his children and his business.

00:15:51.930 --> 00:15:57.859
Karl looked around at his co-workers at his apartment. He suddenly felt a sense of doubt and regret in his life's path.

00:15:58.020 --> 00:15:59.850
He wondered if he did the wrong thing.

00:15:59.850 --> 00:16:03.230
He thought about how he let some stranger make such an important choice for him.

00:16:03.360 --> 00:16:07.759
The worst part wasn't even that his life was bad or good, but that, in this moment,

00:16:07.760 --> 00:16:12.590
it almost felt like it wasn't even his to regret. At the end of the night after mostly everyone went home,

00:16:12.900 --> 00:16:16.250
Karl went on a walk around the city by himself, moderately drunk.

00:16:16.410 --> 00:16:20.629
He walked down the edge of the city along the harbor. He sat down on the same bench

00:16:20.630 --> 00:16:22.910
he sat on many years ago facing the water

00:16:23.130 --> 00:16:27.950
He sat back in the bench looked up at the stars, closed his eyes, and breathed in deeply.

00:16:28.890 --> 00:16:32.390
Suddenly, an old man poked Karl in the shoulder. Karl,

00:16:32.820 --> 00:16:39.589
disoriented, opened his eyes to an old man standing above him. Before Karl could say anything, the old man asked if he was okay.

00:16:39.810 --> 00:16:43.879
Karl having realized he must have passed out told the man he was okay; he just fell asleep.

00:16:44.130 --> 00:16:50.800
The old man sat on the bench a few feet away, as he explained to Karl how it was unsafe to fall asleep in such an area

00:16:51.180 --> 00:16:55.009
"You're likely to get robbed or something." he said. Karl agreed and said it was an accident.

00:16:55.130 --> 00:16:59.060
He explained to the old man that he hadn't been able to get much sleep the last several days,

00:16:59.220 --> 00:17:03.139
because he had been trying to make the decision about whether or not he would be moving to

00:17:03.450 --> 00:17:09.859
Australia. Karl explained the situation about the business, his best friend, his girlfriend, his father and everything else as

00:17:09.990 --> 00:17:12.440
he did, Karl felt a weird sense of clarity.

00:17:12.540 --> 00:17:14.090
He couldn't put his finger on it,

00:17:14.090 --> 00:17:18.259
but he almost felt like he knew what each decision would be like, like he had already lived them.

00:17:18.600 --> 00:17:25.260
But yet, despite this, he still had no clue which one was better. While Karl was thinking about this, suddenly the old man interrupted.

00:17:25.780 --> 00:17:30.119
"What if you just decide right now based on what I choose, someone who basically doesn't know you at all?

00:17:30.370 --> 00:17:34.770
It'll be like flipping a coin but the coin has a brain and understands the situation a little."

00:17:35.410 --> 00:17:42.029
Karl thought for a moment and then politely said, "No, I think I need to decide for myself. Thank you, though."

00:17:42.210 --> 00:17:47.010
"I thought you said you couldn't figure out which choice was right?" said the old man. "How are you gonna get past that?"

00:17:47.590 --> 00:17:49.299
"I guess I'm not"

00:17:49.299 --> 00:17:55.769
replied Karl. "I spent the last week trying to figure out which choice was right, but maybe there isn't a right choice.

00:17:56.080 --> 00:17:58.890
Maybe there's just a choice." "What do you mean by that?",

00:17:59.799 --> 00:18:05.849
interrupted the old man. "Maybe some decisions aren't hard because there's a better option, but because there isn't one.

00:18:06.460 --> 00:18:07.350
This whole time,

00:18:07.350 --> 00:18:12.299
I've been worried about regretting my choice by choosing the wrong thing, as if I could even know what I'm regretting.

00:18:12.760 --> 00:18:17.039
Regret would mean there was a right choice and I made the wrong one, but how could there be a wrong one?

00:18:17.320 --> 00:18:20.490
The only thing I can know is that on the other side of the decision,

00:18:20.490 --> 00:18:23.939
I'll be there. And if I'm there, no matter what path I go,

00:18:24.039 --> 00:18:29.849
there'll always be something to love and there'll always be something to dread, and the only thing to regret would be not making the

00:18:29.850 --> 00:18:34.439
decision for myself. If you pick it, if anyone else picks it, if I leave it up to chance,

00:18:34.900 --> 00:18:39.989
it'll never be mine, or at least, it won't feel like it." "So how do you suppose you choose now?"

00:18:40.510 --> 00:18:47.129
"I guess I do my best, pick one, and move on." replied Karl. "It won't be chance, it won't be certainty.

00:18:47.350 --> 00:18:50.159
It'll be somewhere in between, and it'll be the right choice."

00:18:51.290 --> 00:18:56.290
"So," said the old man with a continued smile, "which are you going to choose?"

